  "worked_solution": "When we have an inequality involving an exponential function with a positive base, we're allowed to multiply or divide both sides by the exponential function.\n\nMultiplying both sides of the given inequality by $(0.6)^{3x-2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4)^{3}}{(0.6)^{3x-2}} & <0 \\\\ (0.6)^{3x-2}\\cdot \\frac{({x}^{2}-4)^{3}}{(0.6)^{3x-2}} & <(0.6)^{3x-2}\\cdot 0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4)^{3} & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-4)^{3}} & <\\sqrt[3]{0} \\\\ {x}^{2}-4 & <0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & <4 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & <\\sqrt{4} \\\\ |x| & <2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality is $-2<x<2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
